Make room in the store before a new entry becomes visible (Mervin PDF patch for MuPDF 1.28.5)

This file modifies MuPDF. The Mervin PDF project changed
source/fitz/store.c on 2026-10-18. scripts/build-mupdf-linux.sh and
scripts/build-mupdf-windows.ps1 apply it to the verified 1.28.5 source
release after patch 0001, before building. Check it again on every
MuPDF update.

fz_store_item puts a new fz_item into the hash table first, with
item->next == item, so the item is not in the LRU list yet. When the
store is full, it then makes room. do_reap and ensure_space release
FZ_LOCK_ALLOC while they free evicted values, and only afterwards does
fz_store_item count the item's size and link it into the list with
touch(). While the lock is released, another thread can find the item
through the hash (fz_find_item, or the duplicate branch of another
fz_store_item call), and its touch() links the item into the list.
fz_filter_store, fz_empty_store or fz_remove_item can then free it
whatever its reference count. The storing thread then runs touch() on
freed memory, or rewrites the removal chain of a filter that has not
freed the item yet.

In Mervin, two render workers can store and find the same pattern tile
(its key names the pattern, the document, the transform and the colour
space) while a closing tab filters the document's tiles out of the
store.

This patch runs the size check before the hash insert, so the item
enters the hash table, has its size counted and is linked into the list
within one hold of FZ_LOCK_ALLOC. fz_hash_insert releases the lock only
to resize the table, before it inserts the item. A store that finds a
duplicate may now have evicted entries for nothing.
newEntryStaysHiddenWhileStoreEvicts in tests/tst_mupdf_store.cpp checks
it.

--- a/source/fitz/store.c
+++ b/source/fitz/store.c
@@ -478,6 +478,58 @@
 	item->prev = item;
 	item->type = type;
 
+	/* If we haven't got an infinite store, check for space within it.
+	 * Do this before the item goes into the hash table. do_reap and
+	 * ensure_space drop the lock while they free evicted values, and an
+	 * item in the hash table but not yet in the list could then be
+	 * found, linked and freed (fz_filter_store, fz_remove_item and
+	 * fz_empty_store free items whatever their refs) before we link it
+	 * ourselves below. If a duplicate turns up below, this eviction was
+	 * not needed. */
+	if (store->max != FZ_STORE_UNLIMITED)
+	{
+		/* FIXME: Overflow? */
+		size = store->size + itemsize;
+		if (size > store->max)
+		{
+			FZ_LOG_STORE(ctx, "Store size exceeded: item=%zu, size=%zu, max=%zu\n",
+				itemsize, store->size, store->max);
+			while (size > store->max)
+			{
+				size_t saved;
+
+				/* First, do any outstanding reaping, even if defer_reap_count > 0 */
+				if (store->needs_reaping)
+				{
+					do_reap(ctx); /* Drops alloc lock */
+					fz_lock(ctx, FZ_LOCK_ALLOC);
+				}
+				size = store->size + itemsize;
+				if (size <= store->max)
+					break;
+
+				/* ensure_space may drop, then retake the lock */
+				saved = ensure_space(ctx, size - store->max);
+				size -= saved;
+				if (saved == 0)
+				{
+					/* Failed to free any space. */
+					/* We used to 'unstore' it here, but that's wrong.
+					 * If we've already spent the memory to malloc it
+					 * then not putting it in the store just means that
+					 * a resource used multiple times will just be malloced
+					 * again. Better to put it in the store, have the
+					 * store account for it, and for it to potentially be reused.
+					 * When the caller drops the reference to it, it can then
+					 * be dropped from the store on the next attempt to store
+					 * anything else. */
+					break;
+				}
+			}
+			FZ_LOG_DUMP_STORE(ctx, "After eviction:\n");
+		}
+	}
+
 	/* If we can index it fast, put it into the hash table. This serves
 	 * to check whether we have one there already. */
 	if (use_hash)
@@ -527,51 +579,6 @@
 		(void)Memento_takeRef(val);
 		val->refs++;
 	}
